// tanh smooths Coulomb friction through zero speed; fabs makes the fan load
// oppose the rotation in either direction.
from <math> import tanh, fabs;

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  Rotational mechanics on a shaft node — inertia, friction, loads, a
//  flexible shaft and an ideal gear
//
//  These parts use the shaft convention of <electromechanical> (PMSM,
//  InductionMachine, the DC machines, BLDC), the force–current analogy:
//
//      V(shaft) = ωm, shaft speed in rad/s         (1 V = 1 rad/s)
//      current  = torque in N·m                    (1 A = 1 N·m)
//      C = inertia (kg·m²),  conductance = viscous friction (N·m·s/rad),
//      L = 1 / torsional stiffness (rad/(N·m)),  power = V·I in W
//
//  A machine's torque is a current INTO its shaft node; a load is anything
//  that draws current OUT of it to ground. Every one-port part below is
//  connected [shaft] and draws its torque from shaft to gnd, so it simply
//  sits on the machine's shaft wire beside the machine, and several of them
//  on one node add up (inertias add, torques add):
//
//      wire shaft;
//      module m    = InductionMachine<...>(...)[a, b, c, shaft];
//      module jl   = Inertia<0.01>()[shaft];
//      module fan  = FanLoad<10, 150>()[shaft];
//
//  A positive load torque opposes positive rotation; with the machine
//  motoring at positive speed, the power ω·T a load absorbs is positive.
//
//  The two-port parts (FlexibleShaft, Gear) join two shaft nodes:
//  [shaft_a, shaft_b], where shaft_a is the driving side by convention.
//
//  Parts
//      Inertia<J>                  [shaft]   J·dω/dt                (C)
//      ViscousFriction<B>          [shaft]   B·ω                    (VCCS; B = 0 ok)
//      CoulombFriction<Tc, w_s>    [shaft]   Tc·tanh(ω/w_s)
//      FanLoad<T_rated, w_rated>   [shaft]   T_rated·(ω/w_rated)·|ω/w_rated|
//      ConstantTorque<T>           [shaft]   T, whatever the speed
//      TorqueStep<T0, T1, t_step>  [shaft]   T0, then T1 from t_step on
//      FlexibleShaft<K, D>         [shaft_a, shaft_b]  K·Δθ + D·Δω
//      Gear<n>                     [shaft_a, shaft_b]  ω_b = ω_a/n, T_a = T_b/n
//
//  Not here, deliberately: backlash and stick-slip (static friction above
//  the Coulomb level, a true stop at zero speed). Both are discontinuities
//  in the force law that the smooth forms below cannot represent honestly;
//  CoulombFriction's tanh lets the shaft creep at any torque below Tc, at a
//  speed of about w_s·T/Tc.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  Inertia
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
//  T = J·dω/dt, a capacitor of J farads from shaft to ground. Stores the
//  kinetic energy ½·J·ω². Under .op the inertia is relaxed like any
//  capacitor: the shaft starts at the DC speed the torques balance at.

module Inertia<double J>()[wire shaft]
{
    C<J>() [shaft, gnd];
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  Viscous friction
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
//  T = B·ω, a conductance B. Written, as in PMSM, as a VCCS sensing its own
//  terminals, so that B = 0 is no torque rather than R<1/0>.

module ViscousFriction<double B>()[wire shaft]
{
    VCCS<B>() [shaft, gnd, shaft, gnd];
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  Coulomb (dry) friction, smoothed through zero speed
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
//      T = Tc·tanh(ω / w_s)
//
//  The ideal Coulomb law Tc·sign(ω) jumps by 2·Tc at ω = 0 — a step in the
//  torque inside some solver step, and an infinite slope for Newton. The
//  tanh replaces the jump by a ramp of width w_s (rad/s): the torque is
//  76 % of Tc at |ω| = w_s, 96 % at 2·w_s and 99.9 % at 4·w_s, and its slope
//  at standstill is Tc/w_s, a viscous friction of that size. Pick w_s small
//  next to the speeds of interest but not so small that Tc/w_s dwarfs the
//  rest of the shaft's damping: w_s = 0.1–1 % of the running speed is
//  typical. Below Tc a holding torque does not hold the shaft: it creeps at
//  ω ≈ w_s·atanh(T/Tc) (see the header: no stiction).

analog module CoulombFriction<double Tc, double w_s>()[wire shaft]
{
    double torque = Tc * tanh(V(shaft) / w_s);
    current_source<>(torque) [shaft, gnd];
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  Fan / pump / propeller load, torque ∝ speed²
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
//      T = T_rated·(ω/w_rated)·|ω/w_rated|
//
//  The affinity law of a centrifugal fan or pump: T_rated at w_rated (rad/s),
//  a quarter of it at half speed, and the power T·ω ∝ ω³. Written with |ω| so
//  that it opposes the rotation in either direction; the torque is
//  continuously differentiable through zero (slope 2·T_rated·|ω|/w_rated²,
//  zero at standstill), so it needs no smoothing.

analog module FanLoad<double T_rated, double w_rated>()[wire shaft]
{
    double x = V(shaft) / w_rated;
    double torque = T_rated * x * fabs(x);
    current_source<>(torque) [shaft, gnd];
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  Constant torque
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
//  T drawn from the shaft at any speed, in a fixed direction — a hoist
//  holding a weight, a conveyor against gravity: an ACTIVE load. It does not
//  reverse with the speed, so a machine that cannot hold it is driven
//  backwards (T > 0 then turns the shaft negative). For a load that always
//  opposes the motion use CoulombFriction.

module ConstantTorque<double T>()[wire shaft]
{
    current_source<T>() [shaft, gnd];
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  Torque step
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
//  T0 before t_step, T1 from t_step on (an active load, like ConstantTorque).
//  The comparison is a time breakpoint, so the variable-step solvers land a
//  step exactly on t_step with or without .zcd (manual, "Time Breakpoints").

analog module TorqueStep<double T0, double T1, double t_step>()[wire shaft]
{
    double torque = T0;
    if (time >= t_step) { torque = T1; }
    current_source<>(torque) [shaft, gnd];
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  Flexible shaft (torsion spring + damper) — the two-mass coupling
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
//      T = K·(θa − θb) + D·(ωa − ωb)        transmitted from a to b
//
//  A spring is an inductor in this analogy (dT/dt = K·Δω): L = 1/K henry,
//  whose branch current is the spring torque. The damper is a conductance D
//  between the two nodes (a self-sensing VCCS, so D = 0 is allowed). Put
//  an Inertia on shaft_b (or a machine, or a load) to make the two-mass
//  system; with J_a and J_b on its ends the shaft resonates at
//
//      f_0 = (1/2π)·√(K·(J_a + J_b)/(J_a·J_b)).
//
//  The twist starts at zero (θa = θb at t = 0). Under .op the spring is a
//  short at DC: both ends turn at one speed, the spring carrying the steady
//  torque. The spring torque is I(<instance>.spring) (positive from a to b);
//  the twist angle is that over K.
//
//  Parameters
//      K   torsional stiffness, N·m/rad (> 0)
//      D   torsional damping, N·m·s/rad (0 allowed)

module FlexibleShaft<double K, double D>()[wire shaft_a, wire shaft_b]
{
    L spring<1.0 / K>()  [shaft_a, shaft_b];
    VCCS<D>()            [shaft_a, shaft_b, shaft_a, shaft_b];
}

// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  Ideal gear
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
//      ω_b = ω_a / n,      T_a = T_b / n            (lossless: ωa·Ta = ωb·Tb)
//
//  n is the ratio of input to output speed: n = 5 is a 5:1 reducer (the
//  output turns 5 times slower with 5 times the torque); a negative n
//  reverses the direction. Seen from shaft_a, an inertia J on shaft_b is
//  J/n², a friction B is B/n², a torque T is T/n.
//
//  It is an ideal transformer: a VCVS sets V(shaft_b) = V(shaft_a)/n, and its
//  branch current — the torque the gear delivers into shaft_b — is reflected
//  back onto shaft_a by a CCCS of gain 1/n. Because shaft_b is set by a
//  source, nothing else may impose the speed of shaft_b (a speed source, a
//  second gear driving it); put the speed source on shaft_a instead. A rigid
//  gear has no inertia, compliance, backlash or loss of its own — add an
//  Inertia, a FlexibleShaft or a ViscousFriction where those matter.
//
//  The torque delivered into shaft_b is −I(<instance>.mesh).

module Gear<double n>()[wire shaft_a, wire shaft_b]
{
    VCVS mesh<1.0 / n>()  [shaft_b, gnd, shaft_a, gnd];
    CCCS<1.0 / n>()       [gnd, shaft_a, mesh];
}
